\section{Watching a Copying Collector Close a Cycle}

For a small heap experiment, use a stopped mutator and two
semispaces. Every pair occupies one modeled slot, and
values distinguish pointers from immediate atoms. From-space
has room in each evacuated object's old slot to record its
new address. Given root slots and a destination semispace large
enough for the live graph, return updated roots and the compact
occupied destination prefix. This is a teaching model of the
Cheney queue, not a reconstruction of the incremental Symbolics
collector. It focuses on a live cycle, for which recognizing
a previously copied node is needed for termination as well
as identity preservation.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input roots $R$, old heap, empty destination
  \Output rewritten roots and compact live heap
  \Function{Forward}{$v$}
    \If{$v$ is not an old-heap pointer}
      \State \Return $v$
    \ElsIf{$v$ is already forwarded}
      \State \Return its recorded destination
    \EndIf
    \State $p\gets\Call{AppendCopy}{v}$
    \State record $v\mapsto p$ \Comment{Before tracing fields}
    \State \Return $p$
  \EndFunction
  \State update every slot in $R$ using \Call{Forward}{}
  \State $s\gets0$
  \While{$s$ is below destination length}
    \State forward both fields at slot $s$ in field order
    \State $s\gets s+1$ \Comment{Length may have grown}
  \EndWhile
  \State \Return updated roots and destination
\end{sudocode}

Let the root point to $A=(B,7)$, with $B=(A,9)$.
Let an unrelated $C$ also point to itself. Forwarding the
root copies $A$ into slot $0$. Scanning slot $0$ copies
$B$ into slot $1$ and changes the first field to that new
address. Scanning slot $1$ follows its first field to old
$A$, discovers the forwarding entry, and writes destination
address $0$. The cursor catches up at $2$. The compact
heap contains the live cycle $0\to1\to0$ and tail atoms
$7$ and $9$; no slot was allocated for $C$. Add a second
root pointing to $B$ and verify that it ends at slot $1$
rather than producing a second copy.

\Cref{fig:visual-157} follows the destination slots in the one-root
run. The unresolved old pointers disappear in scan order.

