Everything below the scan cursor has rewritten fields.
Everything between that cursor and the allocation frontier
is copied exactly once but might still contain old pointers.
Since each append corresponds to one newly discovered live
object, a finite graph cannot keep growing indefinitely.
Cost is linear in the number of roots and visited fields.
The collection cannot proceed this way if the destination
space is too small; that condition needs a separate heap
policy rather than an out-of-range copy.
